# `actuarial::Discount::defined`

[Back to `actuarial::Discount`](../Discount.md)

Tells whether the discount was built by a factory.

## Signature

```cpp
bool actuarial::Discount::defined() const noexcept;
```

## What It Does

True for a `Discount` made by `discount::flat` or `discount::curve`, false for a default-constructed one. The check does not validate the assumption beyond what the factories already did; it only distinguishes a real discount from the empty value, which `factors` and `operators::value` refuse. Call it before using a `Discount` that may be empty, for example one stored in a configuration that is filled in later.

## Returns

`true` if the discount was built by a factory.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: built and empty

A flat discount and a curve are defined; the default value is not.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln("flat:", actuarial::discount::flat(0.04).defined(), " curve:", actuarial::discount::curve(pluss::make_series({1.0, 0.96})).defined(), " default:", actuarial::Discount{}.defined());
}
```

### Example 2: guarding a stored discount

Use a discount only if it was set.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  actuarial::Discount slot;
  out.writeln("before it is set: factors usable:", slot.defined());
  slot = actuarial::discount::flat(0.03);
  out.writeln("after it is set:  factors usable:", slot.defined(), " factors", slot.factors(2).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: a copy keeps the assumption

Copies are values: both are defined and give the same factors.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Discount original = actuarial::discount::flat(0.05);
  const actuarial::Discount copy = original;
  out.writeln("copy defined:", copy.defined(), " same factors:", copy.factors(3).items<double>() == original.factors(3).items<double>());
}
```

